 ## Overview
-This method renders scenes defined by Signed Distance Functions (SDFs) by casting rays from a camera, marching them to surface intersections, and shading the hit points using the Blinn-Phong lighting model [S1, unofficial]. It differs from analytical ray tracing by using iterative distance estimation to handle complex implicit surfaces, and it differs from standard rasterization by computing lighting per-ray rather than per-vertex [S1, unofficial]. It builds on [Distance-field ray marching](21-distance-field-ray-marching.md).
+This method renders scenes defined by implicit surfaces (Signed Distance Fields) by casting rays from a camera, iteratively marching them to a surface, and shading the hit point using the Blinn-Phong reflectance model [S1, unofficial]. It differs from analytical ray tracing by using iterative distance estimation to handle complex, non-analytic geometry, and it differs from pure SDF rendering by applying a specific local lighting approximation (Blinn-Phong) rather than global illumination or complex material models [S1, unofficial].
 
